00:09:16.207 --> 00:09:21.627
Each pick enters at the next open, holds 5 sessions or hits its name stop, and is scored

00:09:21.627 --> 00:09:26.547
against the NASDAQ over that same window, so anyone can reproduce the number from the ticker,

00:09:26.767 --> 00:09:33.427
the date, and the stop. The full history since March 2026 covers 90 trades with a 52% win rate,

00:09:33.947 --> 00:09:40.747
and cumulative profit of $114.00. And that older book is history, not the test of whether these

00:09:40.747 --> 00:09:47.393
rules beat the index. You hold a Canadian dividend stock inside a TFSA that just posted a 3%

00:09:47.393 --> 00:09:52.333
distribution increase on the same morning the overnight rate ticked higher. Do you add on the

00:09:52.333 --> 00:09:57.793
next pay date or do you rerun the numbers first? I rerun them, the account is still tax-free,

00:09:58.233 --> 00:10:02.113
but the new risk-free rate raises the opportunity cost of holding the equity,

00:10:02.553 --> 00:10:07.473
so the same distribution stream is worth less in present-value terms unless growth accelerates

00:10:07.473 --> 00:10:13.373
enough to compensate. Each extra 25 basis points lifts the discount applied to future distributions.

00:10:13.373 --> 00:10:13.573
decisions.

00:10:13.913 --> 00:10:18.153
Professional managers feed the revised rate into the model before they decide whether

00:10:18.153 --> 00:10:19.973
to add on the next distribution date.

00:10:20.713 --> 00:10:26.053
Recalculate the implied internal rate of return or IRR using the updated overnight rate

00:10:26.053 --> 00:10:31.253
plus your required equity premium, and trim the weight if the new IRR falls below target

00:10:31.253 --> 00:10:32.773
even though no taxes do.

00:10:33.373 --> 00:10:38.573
The common misconception is that tax-free status makes rate moves irrelevant inside a TFSA.

00:10:39.093 --> 00:10:39.673
It does not.

00:10:39.673 --> 00:10:45.493
The fix is to rerun the IRR after every bank of Canada decision, using the new overnight

00:10:45.493 --> 00:10:50.673
rate as the floor. Take current yield, add expected annual distribution growth, and

00:10:50.673 --> 00:10:55.593
subtract the new overnight rate plus a 5% equity premium. The figure tells you whether

00:10:55.593 --> 00:11:00.773
the position still clears your minimum required return. That adjustment bites hardest on high

00:11:00.773 --> 00:11:05.613
yield names whose growth already sits close to the overnight rate, because those names lose

00:11:05.613 --> 00:11:12.116
the most present value when the discount ticks up. Open your TFSA list, sort by yield, and run

00:11:12.116 --> 00:11:17.676
that IRR on the top three names with the new overnight rate, plus the 5% equity premium,

00:11:18.176 --> 00:11:24.096
and note which position now sits below your target. Before we wrap, US 3rd quarter GDP and

00:11:24.096 --> 00:11:28.756
September personal consumption expenditures are the next prints the rate path has to digest.

